 sure that  the data that goes into my training  pipeline is of high quality  because if you have issues with the data  uh can be issues from the source maybe  the data  collection process isn't good enough and  you have some um corrupted data that  that can happen  so at this point we start to think about  the quality of the data  of what what goes in into our training  pipeline but also how can we make sure  that the kind the transformations that  we apply to our data  are implemented correctly so this second  level has two objectives the first one  is prevent  low quality data from entering our  training  pipeline and the second one is that we  want to detect issues with our data  transformations and our data  transformations can be something like a  group by and maybe we are doing some  some other  small tweaks to the data and we want to  make sure that those are implemented  correctly  so now let me show you how this looks  like in practice  um let me go to my file  and since we have limited time i'm i'm  not gonna be showing all the files  i'm just gonna be focusing on i have to  change my  branch first okay two integration that's  why i couldn't find the file okay tests  quality  okay so  uh just to give some more context of  what this sample pipeline is about i'm  using the heart disease data set that's  a sample data set and i i got this uh  really nice notebook from kaggle  and then i started just adding some  tests so what's the idea  in our pipeline we have a script that  cleans the raw data so we apply some  some transformations and the output of  that clean script is  our clean data now the definition of  clean data is subjective and it also  depends on the specifics of our data  sets so it's up to us as a data  scientist to define what clean data  means now we may say  for this data set we have an h column  and we have a sex column right that's  how the data is and we may define some  things like okay if this  data set has one person per row and we  have an h column when well one sensible  thing to say is that  all the observations all the values  on this column should be at least zero  right that that's a good rule to have  now we should automate testing for these  kinds of things  because  it might be the case that  we check our data when we are exploring  we are we have some jupyter notebook we  are exploring data and we check that we  say okay everyone in our data set has an  age of  like between 20 and 70 just to say  something right and it makes sense but  what happens if we make some mistake in  our transformations or we get some  corrupted observations  from the data warehouse we have to make  sure that every time we train a model  we have these rules in place because  these are kind of stop checks that  prevent us from training on low quality  data and i'm implementing this with  plumber because it allows me to  run something and then run this function  that checks the output of that script  but you can really implement this with  any framework  just you just have to uh like  have that concept clear you have some  script that produces some output and now  we want to check how the output looks  like now i also mentioned that this is  also this is integration testing and  some people also call it data quality  testing so it's the same thing i also  mentioned that at this point we are  going to start incorporating unit tests  and this is like a really traditional  like well-known concept  um so imagine that you are  developing some data transformations and  that you have a column  that has an integer  for example here  let's imagine that these are the values  that you have for for that specific  column  and then you want to convert those  values  to some human readable representation  and i got these values from the um  from the documentation from this data  set now this is a really simple  transformation so in many cases if you  are just doing like a really simple  stuff uh might not be worth your time to  inve uh to write a test if you have one  or two lines of code right especially if  you are using some other framework like  pandas that you know have correct  implementations of all these things but  whenever you have some custom  transformations or like some really  specific logic it's really important to  write some unit tests and a unit test  course like works like this you have a  function now you have a function that  does something with some input  so to write your test you define some  input  and then you define what's the expected  output of that function  given this input  and then you call that function with  that input  and verify that it's the same thing that  you expected  and if this happens that means your  tests are working if this fails then it  means that you should have like you  should go back to your implementation  and see what's going on so i can show  you how i can run my unit tests i only  have one but you should be implementing  this for all your custom data  transformations  i'm using this library called pythest  and i can execute pythest  and it can run it it runs my my single  test and it says it's working so i'm  happy my implementation is correct so we  can move on okay so let's go back to our  slides and talk about the level number  three so this is where things start to  get serious um and we start to write  some more robust tests so when i talk  about integration testing and data  quality one of the things that i  mentioned is we can check some of the  values in our columns or for example if  it's a categorical variable that we may  only have two or three values and we can  test for that or if it's like an h  column we should expect that to be  larger than a zero  but these are really basic things right  we are not saying we are only making  assumptions about  each individual column but we are not  saying anything about the overall  population  and changes in the data distribution  can break our models so imagine that we  are training this model that predicts  heart disease  right and we have information on let's  say that  focus on this first uh first curve that  we have here and imagine that we have  something like this that our dataset has  people uh who's between  um like the mean of this distribution is  50 50 years old and that's fine we train  a model we evaluate performance and it  works fine  now  we want to retrain our model let's say  we got some new data we got some um more  recent data from let's say a thousand  new people  um  and then our distribution looks like  this  this is a really drastic change and it  it can be valid it can be that simply we  are collecting data in a different way  and that's just how it is we have to  adapt to the change but it can also be  an issue and we don't know and if we  don't know the best thing  um to do  is to make sure that we detect these  types of changes like if we have some  key variables that we need to pay close  attention to  we have to make sure that whenever these  have drastic  changes  we test for that and raise an issue an  error if that's the case  so that's the first part the second part  is that we are going to start testing  our serving pipeline so at this point we  may have a good model a model that we  want to take to production that we want  to use for making predictions so we are  going to start adding these distribution  changes tests but also we are going to  make sure that we can train a model we  can take that model file and then we can  generate predictions so let's see how  the code looks like let me move to the  next branch okay so the first thing let  me show you how the  configuration file for github actions  looks like  first we run our unit tests and the  order is important here because the unit  tests are supposed to be fast so if they  fail we are going to stop execution  right here and it may take like one or  two minutes and that's great we want to  fail fast if if things are breaking we  want to um know as